On Thu, 7 Oct 2004, Thom Burnett wrote:

> In looking at files for S-Plus source code I see a variety of extensions 
> used.
> 
> Source code has been in *.s, *.q, *.cmd, ... files
> Likewise the output files have been in *.txt, *.out
> 
> Is there a generally approved of standard?

You missed *.ssc, quite widely used on Windows.  I have never seen .cmd 
for S files, but I have seen .S.

The preferred extension for S code in .q (for QPE, Quantitative
Programming Environment, once a posited name for an S replacement that
lives on in sqpe.dll and the Sqpe executable on Unix).

CHAPTER under Windows does not recognize .s files, and under Unix they are 
assembler files.  AFAIK only .q works everywhere, and so I think you will 
find it is the most used extension.

For output, it matters a lot less: I use .out but .txt may be convenient 
under Windows.

