Hello.  I am conducting a study in which I must
estimate the 1st derivatives (slopes) of a variable (y) over a range of values
of x, and then compare these derivatives for 24 different species.  Therefore,
I have to use either a cubic spline smoother or a loess smoother.

The trends in the derivative are more sensitive to
the degree of smoothing (equivalent number of parameters, or the model df) than
are the trends in the predicted values, and so I need an objective – or at
least defensible and standardised – way of choosing the smoother df.  I
have read that the default method (the cross-validation score) is not
particularly good and, in simulations that I have done, tends to oversmooth the
1st derivatives.

An alternative strategy is to fit the same data using
successively more equivalent parameters and then compare these using the anova
function.  This is analogous to fitting polynomial regressions of increasing
numbers of terms and testing for significant improvements in fit.

My questions: 

(1)    
Is this alternative strategy reasonable? 
If so, can someone point to a publication justifying it?

(2)    
If not, can someone suggest a better
strategy?  Remember, the goal is to get estimations of the 1st derivatives
that are least biased without oversmoothing.

 

Thanks for any help.

 

